
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Timothy John Smiley, professor emeritus of philosophy at the University of Cambridge, has died.</p>



<span id="more-27272"></span>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="373" height="293" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Smiley-Timothy.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-27273" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Smiley-Timothy.jpg 373w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Smiley-Timothy-300x236.jpg 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Smiley-Timothy-250x196.jpg 250w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 373px) 100vw, 373px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Professor Smiley was known for his work in logic. He is the author (with D.J. Shoesmith) of <em>Multiple Conclusion Logic </em>(1978) and (with Alex Oliver) of <em>Plural Logic </em> (2013), among other works. You can learn more about his writings <a href="https://philpeople.org/profiles/timothy-smiley" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">here</a>.  A festschrift, <em>The Force of Argument: Essays in Honor of Timothy Smiley</em>, was published in 2010.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Smiley retired from Cambridge in 1998 as Knightbridge Professor of Philosophy. He spent his entire career at Cambridge, starting as a fellow of Clare College in 1955, following his undergraduate and graduate education there. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">He died last week. </p>
